heck `g` first: a non-empty `g` other than `d` (including `w`, `m`, `v`) answers status 404, `Content-Type: text/html`, the `error-404.html` body and an `X-Revenant-Note: interval <g> is not reanimated yet` header; these intervals are deferred (Gen 2.7).\n4. Then, if `s` is absent, empty or has no entry in `donor/index.json`, answer status 404, `Content-Type: text/html` and the `error-404.html` body, with no CSV content. Query values arrive percent-decoded, so `s=^GSPC` and `s=%5EGSPC` select the same donor and give identical responses (Gen 2.11, 7.1).\n5. Resolve the start date from `a`, `b`, `c` and the end date from `d`, `e`, `f`. If any one of the three is absent, empty or not `int()`-able (for example `c={{(-1 month).year}}`), the whole date is missing: use the earliest Row_Date of the donor for the start and the latest Row_Date for the end (Gen 2.1, 2.2, 2.3, 2.4, 2.5, 2.6, 5.1, 5.2).\n6. Otherwise months are 0-indexed (`a=0` is January): `year = c + a // 12`, `month = a % 12 + 1`, so 12 rolls over to January of the next year and other out-of-range integers roll the same way (inferred); never reject an out-of-range integer. Clamp the day to `1..days_in_month` (inferred). A year outside 1..9999 counts as a missing date (inferred). Never raise an unhandled exception (Gen 3.1, 3.2).\n7. Split the donor file bytes on `b\"\\n\"`. Keep the Data_Rows whose Row_Date lies in `[start, end]`, both inclusive, each copied byte for byte with no reformatting of any number (a zero Volume stays `000`), in the donor's order (newest first) (Gen 4.2, 4.4, 4.7).\n8. Answer status 200, `Content-Type: text/csv`, with the body Header_Line `Date,Open,High,Low,Close,Volume,Adj Close` + `\\n` + each kept row + `\\n` (Gen 4.1, 4.2, 4.4).\n9. If no Data_Row lies in the range (a start after the end included), answer status 404, `Content-Type: text/html` and the `error-404.html` body (Gen 7.1).\n10. Every response, 404 included, carries exactly one `X-Revenant-Donor` header: `wayback:<host>